				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/petrograd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(455145); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=455145&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609923649" alt="Petrograd" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=455145)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/petrograd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(455145); return false;" >Petrograd</a></span> <span class="odate time_1440735716 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">28 Aug 2015 04:21</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2365488">
									

<p>I'm&#8230; not really on board with this one, for a variety of reasons. Minor disclaimer, I've read some but not all of the Anderson tales/SCPs, and I didn't/don't intend to click any of the links in this article.</p>
<p>Ok, here we go.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>On the first couple readthroughs, I had trouble picturing the concept, particularly what it would look like to an affected person. Even after groking that, I'm still not clear on the transmission characteristics. The interview log was an interesting exploration of the consequences in a specific scenario, but it didn't really help me with either of these issues. The toy-box analogy, for example, didn't really clarify it at all.</p>
<p>The Foundation-originating cognitohazard&#8230; Well, it does makes sense. We definitely see the Foundation using memetic-kill images left and right, and we know these obvious Foundation fronts can operate openly, so why not have them use cognitihazards? Plus, the way this one is set up sounds great&#8230; until you think about it.</p>
<p>The problem is, the way this is implemented is incredibly stupid (in-universe) for several reasons:<br />
1) You're deploying something that is inherently-amnestic resistant and incredibly easily spread on people who are&#8230; who? Members of the public asking the wrong questions? Deliberate infiltrators who are aware of the Foundation, and thus most likely part of a GoI? I just don't ever see a use case where releasing a ravenous infohazard erasing both connections with and the idea of the Foundation is a good idea. Unless you think you can give it to the West Coast Fifthists every month and hope it doesn't cross-contaminate the GOC, UIU, or U.S. government.<br />
I mean, it prefect for Anderson, but who on earth were the Foundation going to use this on?<br />
2) It's simple enough to modify that (assuming you can perceive it or copy the text) you can smudge out SCP and write anything. I mean, Jesus Christ people.<br />
3) It's perfectly understood (they wrote it), but anomalous enough that when it's not being used by the Foundation, it's considered an SCP. This falls somewhere outside of the categories of demi-anomalous tech (e.g., amnestics, GOC Orange Suits) and secret anomaly use (Thaumiels) into a territory of double-standard I'm not comfortable with. If it was clear that Anderson had modified its effects in any way, maybe. But it's not.<br />
4) How are the Foundation members immune to their version? What if they want to recruit someone already infected?</p>
<p>Ultimately, I could maybe see this deployed as a bureaucratic and all-around terrible idea and later revoked, and then ripped off by Anderson. Maybe. I think it's interesting enough that it wouldn't just be wacky incompetent Foundation hijinks. Unfortunately, that's not explored. We're just supposed to take it at face value that the secretary at Smith &amp; Pool Consultants has 40 of these in their desk.</p>
<p>Also, the final letter. On the upside, it's one of my favorite Foundation-kiss-off notes ever, which is impressive because I usually detest them. On the downside, in the context of the article, I have absolutely no idea what any of it means. As I said, for this article, I'm not clicking links or reading tales, so the note for me has no meaning whatsoever. I'm not talking positive things like an air of mystery or an enigmatic character showing off. It's like a postcard from someone I've never met happened to fall on my SCP dossier.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Honestly, I think I'm gonna downvote. It relies too much on an incredibly flawed central premise (a Foundation device useless to the Foundation but prefect for Anderson), some clarity issues, and sections that rely on external content to their detriment.</p>

							</div>

								
					
